WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:14.000
<v Priya (S&OP Lead)>Thanks for jumping on short notice. UNAI flagged a disruption this morning — our APAC supplier V-2207 hit a risk score of 0.78 after the port strike news broke.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:32.000
<v Priya (S&OP Lead)>The agent already re-forecast demand, pulled forward about twelve percent, and rebalanced inventory. It also raised two replacement purchase orders. I want to walk through both before end of day.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:34.000
<v Marcus (Buyer)>Right, the first one is FG-1001, one thousand eighty four units, rerouted to our backup supplier V-9001. Value came in at forty six thousand and seventy dollars, confidence ninety eight percent, so that one executed autonomously — no action needed from us.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:52.000
<v Marcus (Buyer)>The second one is the one I want to flag. FG-1003, five hundred eighty six units from V-3310, value fifty six thousand two hundred fifty six dollars. Confidence was eighty eight percent, which clears our eighty five percent autonomy threshold.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:10.000
<v Marcus (Buyer)>But it still got held for a human approver, because it's above the fifty thousand dollar value gate. So even though the agent was confident, the policy rule kicked in and routed it to me for sign-off.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:05.000
<v Priya (S&OP Lead)>Good, that's exactly the governance behavior we wanted. Can you confirm the primary driver it cited for that decision?

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:22.000
<v Marcus (Buyer)>Yes — stock-out revenue risk, eighty six percent of the decision weight. The counterfactual note says a PO at or below fifty thousand would have gone through on its own; this one just needed my sign-off because of the dollar amount.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:40.000
<v Priya (S&OP Lead)>Understood. Approve it today so we don't slip the replenishment window, then let's close this out.
